I've read the phase's delivery, the shipped config, all three touched test files, and both delivery documents. Here is what I found.

FINDINGS:
1. [MAJOR] phases/phase-084/FINDINGS.md:1990 — The round-15 account of the defect it fixed does not match the code it quotes, and the new test's first assertion pins nothing. FINDINGS.md:1993-1996 quotes the pre-fix loop body as `if let Err(e) = complete() { failure = Some(e); }`, which **overwrites** `failure` on every failing attempt — so the pre-fix refusal already named the *last* failure whenever the attempts failed; the only stale case was "an attempt failed and a later attempt *succeeded*" (the `Some(e)` was never cleared). FINDINGS.md:1999-2002 and REPORT.md:219-221 nevertheless claim that "an attempt that failed for one reason, followed by an attempt that failed for another ... left the refusal naming a problem that had already gone away", and REPORT.md:219 ("both kept the FIRST attempt's failure across the whole loop") is false for the same reason. Why it matters: the new test's first assertion (tests/unreviewed_work_ack_test.rs:1488-1491, tests/checkpoint_ancestry_test.rs:1344-1347) is exactly the "every attempt fails with a distinct message" scenario, and under the quoted pre-fix code it PASSES — so it pins nothing about the fix; only the second half (tests/unreviewed_work_ack_test.rs:1499-1521 / tests/checkpoint_ancestry_test.rs:1354-1377) can fail, and that is the one the report's own mutation paste (FINDINGS.md:2033-2034) shows failing. The delivery ships an assertion and a narrative about a behaviour change that did not happen. Fix: correct FINDINGS.md:1990-2002 and REPORT.md:218-223 to describe the real pre-fix defect (a failed attempt followed by a *successful* one), and either make the first assertion discriminating or state that it pins behaviour the old code already had. VERIFIED: traced the pre-fix code quoted verbatim at FINDINGS.md:1993-1996 against the fixture at tests/unreviewed_work_ack_test.rs:1474-1491 — under `failure = Some(e)` on each failing attempt the last failing attempt's message is the one retained, so the assertion holds identically under old and new code.
2. [MINOR] tests/agent_permissions_test.rs:1257 — `edge_duplicate_permission_key_resolves_to_the_last_value` cannot fail with respect to the behaviour it names. Its fixture at line 1258 declares `"edit":"allow","edit":"allow"` — the same value twice — so the surviving value is `"allow"` under first-wins resolution and under last-wins resolution alike, and the assertion at lines 1261-1264 (`error.contains("`agent.reviewer.permission.edit` is \"allow\"")`) holds either way. The doc comment at lines 1253-1255 claims the test is "pinned so a hand-merged file cannot hide behind the first occurrence", and REPORT.md:1322 cites it as covering "the boundary of last-wins resolution". Fix: make the fixture `"edit":"deny","edit":"allow"` so only a last-wins resolution reports `"allow"` (the round-15 test at tests/agent_permissions_test.rs:1580 does discriminate, because its two `agent` blocks differ).
3. [MINOR] phases/phase-084/REPORT.md:1346 — The Gates section contradicts its own table and its own pasted output. The table at REPORT.md:1338 says `cargo test --all-targets` = "1967 passed", the pasted gate run at REPORT.md:1388 says "PASS cargo test: 1967 passed, 0 failed", and the sentence immediately below the table says "it is the number the gate itself printed: `PASS cargo test: 1963 passed, 0 failed`". 1963 is the previous round's tree — round 15 added exactly four tests, so 1963 + 4 = 1967. The same stale 1955/1963 pair is pasted a second time, verbatim, at REPORT.md:1416-1445 (duplicating REPORT.md:856-886), so the document carries the stale evidence twice and presents it as this round's measurement. Fix: change REPORT.md:1346-1347 to 1967 and delete or re-measure the duplicated block at REPORT.md:1416-1445.
4. [MINOR] phases/phase-084/REPORT.md:899 — Three `diff substance` numbers are printed for one gate run against one base and are not reconciled. REPORT.md:899 pastes "PASS diff substance: +471 -61" for a run "re-run against the tree as it ships"; REPORT.md:1378 pastes "PASS diff substance: +405 -13" for the full gate run against the same base `1d73e8c` (set at REPORT.md:1359); the report's own round-15 table (REPORT.md:283-287) puts the shipping tree at +322 −6 excluding REPORT.md and leaves REPORT.md's own row as "—" (REPORT.md:286), so no number in the document reconciles the two pastes with the tree it claims to describe. The deletions figure moves 13 → 61, which only the document's own rewriting can explain, and REPORT.md:917-922 and REPORT.md:928-936 each explain one paste without reconciling them. Fix: quote one measurement — `git diff --numstat 1d73e8c` against the shipping tree — and cite that single number; that command is what would settle it.
